2027-01-04T13:43:35  thought   A concrete ask I can just fulfil. A routine that's genuinely safe and useful — no neck circles, include the upper-trap/levator and chest work since most desk neck pain comes from rounded shoulders. Add the red-flag line briefly, then note the sore neck in them and keep the daily rhythm off-peak.

2027-01-04T13:43:35  message   To the owner:
Five minutes, no equipment. Move slowly, stay well inside pain — a stretch should feel like a pull, not a stab.

1. Chin tucks — 1 min. Sit or stand tall. Slide your chin straight back (make a double chin), hold 5 sec, release. 8 reps. This is the most useful one here; it undoes the forward-head thing.

2. Ear to shoulder — 1 min. Drop right ear toward right shoulder, let the weight of your head do it — don't pull. 30 sec each side. Breathe out into it.

3. Look over each shoulder — 45 sec. Turn slowly, hold 4 sec at the end of range, 5 turns each side.

4. Levator stretch — 45 sec. Turn your head 45 degrees to the right, then look down toward your left armpit. 20-30 sec each side. This is the one that gets the sore spot at the top of the shoulder blade.

5. Doorway chest opener — 1 min. Forearms on the doorframe, elbows at shoulder height, step one foot through and lean. 30 sec. Tight chest pulls the head forward; this is doing neck work without touching your neck.

6. Shoulder rolls and squeezes — 30 sec. 10 rolls back, then 10 slow squeezes of the shoulder blades together, holding 3 sec each.

Skip full neck circles — circling under load is the one thing that reliably annoys a sore neck.

If it's still sore in a week, or you get numbness, tingling or pain running down an arm, that's a physio question rather than a stretching one.

Want me to save this as a file, or set a reminder to actually do it?

