2027-01-04T18:23:10  thought   Nothing to look up, nothing to check. Just say something good. The strongest fact is the causal one — the heart that stops when they swim — then RNA editing, then close with the arm-neurons thing. Keep it conversational, not an infodump.

2027-01-04T18:23:10  message   To the owner:
Octopuses have three hearts, and the main one stops beating when they swim. The two small ones keep pumping blood through the gills, but the big systemic heart takes a break — which is why swimming exhausts them and they'd almost always rather crawl. They also have blue blood, because they carry oxygen on copper rather than iron.

The one I find genuinely strange: they edit their own RNA. Most animals keep DNA as the fixed script and RNA as a faithful copy of it. Octopuses routinely rewrite the RNA copy instead, swapping letters to produce proteins that aren't in their genes at all. They run a bit cooler as a result, but it buys them variation on the fly — a way to adapt without waiting for evolution.

And roughly two thirds of their neurons are in their arms, not their head. An arm that's been separated can still reach for food and pass it toward where the mouth would be. Which makes you wonder what it's like to be one — eight limbs each doing a fair bit of its own thinking, and one central octopus trying to keep them coordinated.

They also taste with their skin, and they die young: most live about a year, and the female stops eating while she guards her eggs, then dies as they hatch.

