Here's what's true: you've been at this long enough that you're now reading the same cells three times and calling it work. That's not grit, that's diminishing returns with a guilt problem attached. Nothing you do in the next hour will be as good as what you'd do tomorrow morning with a coffee in you, and you'll pay for the extra hour twice — once now, once tomorrow when you're foggy and slower at the exact thing you stayed up for.

The people who get through big piles of dull work aren't the ones who outlast the pile. They're the ones who stop before they're useless, and come back without dread because they didn't ruin last night over it.

So: close the laptop. Not because you're beaten, because you're done for today and there's a difference. Tomorrow it's the same file, your brain's fresh, and you'll spot the thing you've been staring past for an hour in about ninety seconds.

One thing before you shut it — write down the single next step. One line. Then you don't have to hold it in your head overnight.

