2027-01-05T03:59:26  message   From the owner (sent 2027-01-05T03:59:13+00:00):
Help me word a text declining a party invite without sounding rude.

2027-01-05T03:59:36  reasoning Reasoned (570 chars).
A small writing task. I don't know the party details — who invited them, how close, whether they want to give a reason. Give a couple of options at different warmth levels, plus the principle. Ask which fits if needed, but better to offer and let them pick.

Keep it short, it's late.

The key craft point: decline the invitation, not the person; be specific about the warmth; don't over-explain or invent an excuse that invites negotiation.

Give three versions: warm/friendly, neutral/polite, brief. And note the "no reason needed" principle plus RSVP-by deadline tip.

2027-01-05T03:59:36  thought   Writing task, short. Give three versions at different warmth levels plus the one craft point that matters — decline the event, not the person; don't invent an excuse you'll have to defend. Ask who it's from if they want it tuned.

2027-01-05T03:59:36  message   To the owner:
The trick is to decline the event, not the person — and not to hand them a reason they can argue with. A vague, warm no reads better than a detailed excuse.

Friendly, if you'd genuinely like to see them another time:

"Thanks so much for the invite — I'm going to have to give it a miss, I've got too much on that week. Gutted to miss it. Let's get a drink/coffee just the two of us sometime soon?"

Warm but lower effort, if it's someone you like but don't see much:

"Thank you for thinking of me — I can't make it, I'm afraid, but I hope it's a great night. Send my best to everyone."

Short and clean, for an acquaintance or something you don't want to invite a follow-up about:

"Thanks for the invite! I won't be able to make it, but have a lovely time."

Two things that make the difference: don't apologise more than once (a string of "so sorrys" makes it feel like a bigger deal than it is), and don't invent a specific excuse — "I'm busy that week" is unassailable, "my cousin's visiting" invites "what about the weekend after?".

Who's it from? If it's a close friend I'd tune it differently — and if they've asked for a definite yes or no by a date, worth replying before then rather than leaving them hanging.

