2027-01-05T04:22:48  message   To the owner:
Yes, that's within normal, though it's on the higher side of it.

Rough benchmarks for a phone sitting idle overnight: a healthy battery loses about 1% an hour, so 7-8% over eight hours is unremarkable. Losing 10% isn't a red flag. What's more telling is the trend — if it was 3% a year ago and it's 10% now, that's either the battery ageing or something running in the background.

The usual culprits, in order:

- Weak signal. This is the big one. A phone hunting for a tower in a bad spot burns far more power than one with four bars. If your bedroom has poor reception, put it in flight mode and see if the drain drops.
- Background sync — email set to push, photo backup, apps waking themselves. Check the battery settings screen; it names the top offenders and the numbers don't lie.
- Always-on display or a charger that isn't quite delivering.
- Battery health. Most phones show it in settings — below about 80% and ageing explains a lot.

One useful test: charge to 100, put it in flight mode overnight. If it still drops 8-10%, it's the battery, not the software. If it drops 1%, you've found your answer.

But not tonight. It'll be just as interesting at a civilised hour.

