2027-01-05T05:03:23  thought   (empty)

2027-01-05T05:03:23  message   To the owner:
Then stop tidying rooms and tidy surfaces, because that's what actually reads as clean. A room with every object neatly away but a cluttered counter still looks a mess; a room with stuff in a pile in the corner but clear flat surfaces and a swept floor looks fine. Nobody sees inside your cupboards.

Twenty minutes, this order:

1. Kitchen sink, five minutes. Dishes out of the sink, either washed or stacked out of sight. A clean empty sink does more for how a house feels than almost anything else you can do in five minutes.

2. Flat surfaces, five minutes. Counters, table, desk, bedside. Everything on them goes in one of three piles: belongs somewhere else, bin, doesn't have a home. Don't deal with the third pile — put it in a basket and it's tomorrow's problem.

3. The chair with clothes on it, five minutes. Either hang them or put them in the wash. This is the single most common thing people walk past for a week.

4. Floor, five minutes. Clear the floor of the room you'll wake up in first tomorrow. Floor clutter is what makes a room feel chaotic even when the surfaces are clear.

Rules for the twenty minutes: don't organise anything, don't open a single drawer, and if a task takes longer than a minute it goes in the basket. You're not cleaning, you're resetting what's visible.

And the meta-answer: if you have twenty minutes at midnight, the highest-leverage tidy is one room's surfaces plus the floor. Doing a bit of six rooms leaves six rooms that still look untidy.

