Content: 4am waking (sleep maintenance insomnia) has a few real causes: alcohol, blood sugar dip, temperature, caffeine half-life, stress/cortisol, and the "if you can't sleep get up" rule. Non-hygiene suggestions:
1. Alcohol — big one, half-life, fragmentation in second half of night.
2. Blood sugar — late dinner, carb-heavy, or light dinner; a small protein snack before bed can help if you wake hungry-ish with a racing mind.
3. Temperature — core temp drops then rises ~3-4am; bedroom too warm.
4. Caffeine cutoff calc — half-life ~5-6h, quarter-life; last coffee at 2pm = 25% at 2am. Also alcohol.
5. The 4am cortisol surge is normal; the problem is usually the reaction to it. If you wake at 4 and it's the same time daily, that's a pattern — clock-watching makes it worse; the rule is get up after ~15-20 min and do something dull in dim light, come back.
6. CBT-I is the actual evidence-based treatment; also "sleep restriction" — compress time in bed to match actual sleep, builds pressure. That's the single most effective non-hygiene intervention.
7. If they wake to urinate — fluid timing, or prostate/etc.
8. Check: does the waking come with racing mind (anxiety) or calm boredom? Different fixes. Also screen: blackout—no, that's hygiene.
9. If it's 4am and they can't get back, don't lie there — anxiety conditioning. Also: not checking the clock.
10. Sleep apnea: waking at 4, snoring, dry mouth, morning headache — worth a GP visit. That's the "sane suggestion" that isn't hygiene.

Also: could be age-related normal (biphasic sleep / segmented sleep is historically normal). Honest reframe: waking isn't the problem; the distress is. One wake of 20 min is normal.

