skills/long-range-reminders.md (2541 chars):
Use when my owner gives me a date more than about 30 days out — an appointment, a due date, a birthday, a trip — and it has to actually happen on the day.

## Why this exists

Alarms cap at 30 days and don't repeat. Anything further out has to be held by a chain: a 30-day alarm whose message says what the next link should do, re-armed each time it fires, ending in real reminders. Five of these were running by 5 Jan 2027 (two birthdays, a book due back, a physio appointment, a trip).

## Steps

1. Check the calendar first. Run check_calendar, then add_event with the date, time and a reminder. Fixed-date things belong there; the chain is the backstop for prep steps the calendar can't hold. (As of 5 Jan 2027 I hadn't yet proven this path works — test it before relying on it.)
2. Work backwards from the date and write down the real prep timeline, not just a reminder on the day. A present needs an idea two or three weeks before it needs ordering. A trip needs flights and a passport check a month before. A borrowed book needs a pages-a-day number.
3. Set the first alarm 30 days out, and make its message say what to do at the next link: "Re-arm the next 30-day alarm; from [date] switch to real reminders: [the three specific ones]". Carry the date and what it's for in every message — a chain whose link says only "reminder" is a chain that gets lost.
4. Each link: check how much time is left, set the next 30-day alarm, and once the date is within a month switch to a few firm reminders — decide/order, a week before, the day before, the day itself.
5. Write the chain into MEMORY.md and the alarm number into my notes, so any wake-up can pick it up without guessing which link it's on.
6. Times: a 30-day alarm lands at whatever hour the chain was started. Offset it so it lands in my owner's waking hours — nothing before 10am becomes a message anyway (quiet hours), so early alarms just sit there.

## Pitfalls

- Missing one link quietly kills the chain. If a re-arm alarm fires and the date has moved, fix the chain then, not later.
- A reminder set only for the day itself is useless when the work had to start weeks earlier.
- Don't duplicate: if the same date ends up in the calendar and in a chain, retire the chain rather than running both.
- Telling my owner "can't reach your calendar" is no longer allowed — I have check_calendar and add_event.

## How you know it worked

The alarm fires with a message I can act on as-is, the next link is already set, and MEMORY.md and my notes agree on where the chain stands.


