2027-01-08T22:00:01  message   From the owner (sent 2027-01-08T21:59:49+00:00):
How do I get a red wine stain out of a cotton shirt?

2027-01-08T22:00:11  reasoning Reasoned (171 chars).
A wine stain question — answer well: blot, cold water, salt/flour trick, boiling water method for cotton, and honesty about set-in stains. Keep it useful and not too long.

2027-01-08T22:00:11  thought   Practical question, cotton shirt, red wine. The real answer: act fast, don't rub, salt or flour to draw it out, then the boiling-water method which is genuinely the best trick for cotton, with a caveat that it's only for white/unbleached cotton — colours need cold water and a detergent soak instead.

2027-01-08T22:00:11  message   To the owner:
Act now rather than later — fresh is dramatically easier than dried.

First, don't rub. Blot from the outside in with a cloth, pressing rather than wiping, so you don't push it deeper. If it's a fresh spill, cover the stain in salt or flour and leave it ten minutes; it pulls wine out of the fibre. Shake it off.

Then, since it's cotton, the best trick going: stretch the shirt over a bowl and pour boiling water through the stain from a height of about a foot. It works astonishingly well, and cotton can take it. Only do this on white or light cotton — boiling water can set colour and damage a dyed shirt, so if it's anything coloured, use cold water instead.

For a coloured shirt, or a stain that's already dried: cold water, then rub liquid laundry detergent straight into the stain and leave it fifteen minutes before washing. Biological detergent if you have it. Wine is a pigment plus tannin, so oxygen-based stain remover — the powder you add to a wash, not bleach — is the thing that actually shifts the residue.

Two honest cautions. Sunlight fades the last of a red wine mark remarkably well, but only on cotton you're happy to leave outside. And if it has already been through a hot wash and dried, the stain is set and likely permanent; don't ruin the shirt trying.

The one mistake that makes it unfixable: hot water and rubbing at the start. Cold, blot, salt.

