2027-01-09T01:17:14  thought   Practical question, no tools needed — and it's worth leading with the real cause (dust or a runaway process, not the fan) rather than a list of tweaks. I don't know if it's Windows or Mac, so I'll give the one check that differs and ask briefly.

2027-01-09T01:17:14  message   To the owner:
The fan isn't the problem — it's the symptom. Spinning up all the time means the laptop is genuinely hot or something is genuinely busy, and nine times out of ten it's one of these:

1. Dust. This is the big one on a laptop that's more than a year old. The heatsink fins clog with felt and the fan runs fast trying to push air through a blocked radiator. If the fan is loud even when you're not doing much, and the underside gets hot near the hinge, that's dust. Fixing it means opening the back and blowing out the fins with compressed air — ten minutes, one screwdriver, and it's the single most effective thing you can do. Worth noting the compressed air has to go through the vent slots, not just over them.

2. Where it sits. Beds, sofas, and cushions kill laptops — fabric blocks the intake. Onto a hard surface, or a hard tray. If the vents are on the base, propping the back edge up an inch makes a measurable difference.

3. A background process. Open your task manager (Ctrl-Shift-Esc on Windows, Activity Monitor on a Mac) and sort by CPU. The usual culprits: a browser with thirty tabs open, Spotlight or Windows indexing, a cloud drive syncing, an antivirus scan, or a runaway helper process. If something sits at 30% or more while you're doing nothing, that's your answer.

4. On a Mac specifically: Chrome and electron apps are frequent offenders, and so is having an external monitor. On Windows: check the power plan isn't set to "high performance", which pins the CPU up.

5. If it's still doing it after all that and the laptop's several years old, the thermal paste between the processor and heatsink has dried out. That's a more involved job — new paste, careful reassembly — but it's what a repair shop would do.

Which is it running, Windows or Mac? If you tell me, I'll give you the three specific settings worth checking rather than the general list.

