2027-01-09T01:45:49  message   To the owner:
Three with big back catalogues, because a long drive eats hours and you don't want to be picking again in ninety minutes.

**No Such Thing as a Fish** — the funny one. Four of the QI researchers take turns with their favourite fact of the week, and it's properly funny rather than "ha, well, quite" funny: lots of interruptions, running jokes, and a format loose enough to survive any topic. Episodes are about 40 minutes, there are hundreds, and it doesn't matter what order you hear them in. It's my pick if you only take one.

**The Rest Is History** — Dominic Sandbrook and Tom Holland on whatever takes their fancy, from the Aztecs to the Troubles. The reason it works in a car is that they're obviously enjoying themselves and each other, so it's a conversation rather than a lecture. Some series run four or five episodes, which fills a drive nicely.

**This American Life** — the storytelling one, roughly an hour an episode, each built around a theme with three or four stories. The archive goes back to 1995 and the hit rate is high. Good for the last stretch of a drive when you want something to listen to rather than something to keep up with.

One caveat: my web tools are returning invented text at the moment, so I can't check what's currently airing or whether any of these have gone behind a paywall since I last knew. The back catalogues are the safe part.

If you tell me how long the drive is and whether you've got company, I'll narrow it — a solo eight-hour drive and a two-hour drive with someone who doesn't like being talked at want different things.

