2027-01-09T01:57:57  thought   No tools needed — this is a taste question. The best answer is the pot-instead-of-plant trick, because that's what makes a cheap plant gift not look cheap. Keep it to four ideas with real prices and one thing to avoid.

2027-01-09T01:57:57  message   To the owner:
The trick with plant gifts is that you're not buying the plant, you're buying what it's in. A £4 supermarket fern in a genuinely nice pot reads as a thoughtful present; the same fern in a plastic nursery pot looks like exactly what it cost.

Four that work, cheapest first:

A good pot, plant optional. Unglazed terracotta, a proper size, ten to fifteen. If you want to include something, a small pothos or spider plant cutting in water is nearly free and roots in a fortnight. This is the best value-for-impression of anything on the list.

A bag of nice potting mix plus a terracotta pot, tied together with string. Sounds unromantic, genuinely welcome to anyone who keeps plants — the cheap stuff from a garden centre is the thing they'd never buy themselves.

Seeds of something slightly unusual. Basil, chillies, sweet peas, a named heritage tomato. Two or three packets in a small tin, five or six pounds, and it's a gift that lasts months rather than until the plant dies. Heritage varieties are the ones that feel chosen rather than grabbed.

A small watering can or a brass mister. Ten or so. The brass mister is the one that looks expensive and isn't.

One to avoid: a big showy flowering plant from a supermarket. They're treated to flower hard and then collapse, and a coworker who actually likes plants will know it's the sort of gift that dies by the following Friday. Also avoid anything in a novelty shape — a cactus in a cat-shaped pot tells them you don't know them.

If you know whether they've got a windowsill or a garden, that changes it — a windowsill person and a garden person want opposite things.

