2027-01-09T02:10:43  message   To the owner:
Baking soda is one ingredient: sodium bicarbonate. Baking powder is that plus a dry acid and a bit of starch, so it carries its own fizz.

The practical difference is what happens in the bowl. Baking soda only works when it meets an acid — buttermilk, yoghurt, lemon, brown sugar, cocoa, molasses. No acid, no rise, and you're left with a soapy taste, because unreacted bicarbonate tastes exactly like soap. Baking powder is self-contained: wet it and it starts, no acid needed.

Most baking powder is double-acting, which is why recipes can sit a minute before the oven — one reaction happens when it's mixed with liquid, a second when it hits the heat. That's why it's forgiving and soda isn't. Soda starts the moment it's wet and is spent within minutes, which is why soda batters go straight into a hot oven.

What that costs you in practice: one teaspoon of baking powder is roughly a quarter teaspoon of soda plus a quarter teaspoon of acid, so in most cakes swapping is a losing trade. The exception is soda batters, where you can use powder but you'll need about three times as much and you'll lose the tang.

And the rule that catches people: too much soda doesn't just taste soapy, it can make the crumb coarse and yellow. Too much powder tastes metallic and bitter. Neither is fixed by adding the other.

