The owner asks: what to know before buying a used electric kettle. Give a practical answer, honest, with real checks. No tools needed.

Key points: limescale in a used kettle is cosmetic and descaling is easy (citric acid or vinegar). The important checks: the heating element — concealed flat element vs exposed coil; exposed coils are older, harder to clean and scale collects on them. Plastic kettles absorb flavours — a used plastic one may permanently taste of whatever was boiled. Cordless kettles with a base: check the contacts are clean, not corroded. Thermostat/auto-shutoff — check it actually clicks off at the boil; that's the safety-critical part and the main failure. Lid and hinge. Scale filter. Speed of boil. Check for rust — a stainless kettle with rust spots at the seams is a sign of poor quality steel; a plastic one doesn't have that. Check the interior for cracking. Check whether it's a "quick boil" only boils enough — no. Check capacity. Price: a new basic kettle is cheap, so a used kettle doesn't save much — if it's more than a third of the new price, just buy new.

Also: warranty, no way to know how much it was used. Check the seal around the base of the interior. Check the water level window for leaks. Smell test — boil water and taste it. Check whether it switches off when tipped/empty (dry boil protection).

Also electrical safety: check the plug and cord for damage, no cracked casing. Ask why they're selling. Estate sales.

Lead with the one thing: the price ceiling — a new one is £15-25, so don't pay much. And the two-minute test at the seller's place: fill it, boil it, watch it switch off, taste the water.

Keep it organized but not bloated. Plain text, no emojis.

2027-01-09T16:04:52  thought   (empty)

2027-01-09T16:04:52  message   To the owner:
Main thing first: the economics are against you. A basic new kettle costs very little — a decent one is fifteen to twenty pounds, and a good stove or stainless one not much more. So a used kettle is only worth it if it's nearly free, or if it's a genuinely better object than you'd buy new (a proper stainless or a nice brand). Don't pay half the new price for something with a stranger's history.

What actually matters, in order:

The auto shut-off. This is the safety-critical part and the usual failure. At the seller's, fill it, boil it, and watch it click off on its own. If it doesn't, or it keeps boiling, walk away — that's a fire risk you can't repair cheaply.

The element. A concealed flat plate at the bottom is what you want; old exposed coils are hard to descale and limescale cakes onto them.

The inside. If it's plastic and it looks cloudy, scratched or smells of anything, the smell doesn't come out — boiling water drives flavour into plastic permanently. A used plastic kettle that's had coffee or soup through it is a used plastic kettle. Stainless or glass doesn't have that problem, but check stainless for rust at the seams and around the element — rust there means cheap steel, not just age.

The base contacts. Cordless kettles (on that little power base) have sprung contacts that corrode if it lived somewhere damp. Look for green or black on the metal, and check it doesn't wobble.

The lid, hinge and filter. Scale filter behind the spout — if it's missing or bunged up, that's a small annoyance, not a dealbreaker. Lid that doesn't seat properly means steam in your face.

The smells and the scale are the same fix, incidentally: white vinegar or citric acid, half an hour, and most of it goes.

Ask how old it is and why they're selling. "Bought a new kitchen" is fine; "it keeps tripping" isn't. Check the plug and cord for any cracking, and be wary of a kettle with no brand name on it at all — cheap imported ones are the ones that fail dangerously.

If you want the shorter version: bring it to the boil and watch it switch off, then smell the inside. Those two checks catch nearly everything worth catching.

