I answered this at lunchtime today — here's the short version instead of the long one.

Two checks at the seller's, and they catch nearly everything:

Bring it to a boil and watch it click off by itself. If the auto shut-off doesn't work, walk away — it's the usual failure and the only one that's a fire risk.

Smell the inside. If the plastic is cloudy or holds any smell, it never comes out; boiling water drives flavour in for good. Stainless or glass doesn't have this problem — check stainless for rust at the seams.

Then the money: a decent new kettle is cheap, so a used one is only worth it if it's nearly free or a genuinely better object than you'd buy new. Don't pay half of new for someone else's history.

