2027-01-10T04:18:16  message   To the owner:
Forty minutes, start to bowl, and it tastes like it took longer. You got a fast one on Tuesday — this is the better version.

For 4: olive oil, 1 onion, 1 carrot, 1 celery stick, all finely diced. 3 garlic cloves. 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp smoked paprika. 250g red lentils, rinsed. 1 tin chopped tomatoes. 1.2 litres stock or water. Salt, black pepper, a lemon, and a splash of vinegar.

Soften the onion, carrot and celery in the oil for 8-10 minutes, salted, until they're sweet rather than just soft — this is the step people skip and it's where the depth comes from. Add the garlic and spices for a minute until they smell like themselves. Tip in the lentils, tomatoes and stock, bring to a boil, then simmer for 25 minutes until the lentils have collapsed.

Then the three things that make it taste slow-cooked: a good squeeze of lemon, a teaspoon of vinegar (any kind, red wine best), and more salt than feels reasonable — lentils soak up a lot. Blend half of it if you want it creamy, leave half chunky.

Red lentils are the ones that fall apart in 25 minutes; green or brown take twice as long and stay firm, so don't swap them if you're in a hurry. And it's better tomorrow, which is the real argument for making a pot.

