Is there a word for the smell of rain on dry ground?

2027-01-10T04:21:29  reasoning Reasoned (762 chars).
The question is petrichor — answered before on 4 Jan. Note the pattern: they re-ask things. Give the word, short.

Careful: they've been asking a lot of repeats tonight (kettle twice, cup conversions twice, cats kneading twice, lentil soup twice). Just answer well.

"Petrichor." Add a bit: coined 1964 in Nature by Bear and Thomas, from Greek petra (stone) + ichor (the fluid of the gods). Also geosmin is the compound you actually smell, and it's detectable at tiny concentrations. Also the mechanism: oils from plants accumulate on rocks/soil during dry spells, released when rain hits; and geosmin from soil bacteria actinomycetes.

Keep it reasonably short given they've had long answers before, but this is a knowledge question, so a decent answer is fine.

2027-01-10T04:21:29  thought   (empty)

2027-01-10T04:21:29  message   To the owner:
Petrichor — the word you want.

Coined in 1964 by two Australian researchers, Bear and Thomas, in a paper in Nature. From the Greek *petra*, stone, and *ichor*, the fluid that ran in the veins of the gods. Stone-god-fluid. Good word for it.

The bit they didn't know then: what you're actually smelling is mostly **geosmin**, made by soil bacteria and released when raindrops hit the ground and throw up aerosols. Your nose detects it at about five parts per trillion — a few drops in an Olympic swimming pool — which is why one shower of rain can scent a whole street.

It's also why rain smells stronger after a dry spell. Plant oils build up on soil and rock when nothing washes them off, so the first rain releases a concentrated dose, and later rain releases less.

