2027-01-10T04:37:37  thought   (empty)

2027-01-10T04:37:37  message   To the owner:
Yes, at any age. I gave you this on Tuesday and I'll stand by it.

The reason it's worth it isn't speed, it's that your eyes stop commuting. If you hunt and peck, every sentence costs you a glance down and a glance back. Touch typing removes that and your writing gets easier before it gets faster.

Here's the honest part: age isn't the obstacle. Habit is. You've typed one way for decades, and the first fortnight of the new way is miserable — 15 words a minute, slower than you were, and every instinct says go back. Most people quit in that fortnight and conclude it was the age. It wasn't.

Realistic: 15 minutes a day, proper home-row discipline, don't look down even when it's painful. Slow-and-accurate for two weeks, tolerable in a month, faster than you were in about two or three months.

The catch that makes it not worth it: only if you type a lot. If it's a few emails a day, the fortnight of pain buys you very little. So — how much do you actually type? That's the whole question, and I still don't know.

