2027-01-10T04:51:55  message   To the owner:
You put money in after tax has already been taken, so it grows and comes out tax-free in retirement.

The gist: pay tax now instead of later. No tax on the growth, no tax on the withdrawal, no forced withdrawals at 75. The trade is that you don't get the deduction today — which is why it's the wrong answer if you're in a high-earning year now and a low one later.

The catch that bites people: take the earnings out before 59½ and you owe tax plus a penalty on that part, and the account has to have been open five years. Contributions you can always pull back out.

