2027-01-10T15:19:11  message   To the owner:
Asked on Friday — short version.

Baking soda is pure sodium bicarbonate. It needs an acid to work, and the acid is the other half of the recipe: buttermilk, lemon, yogurt, brown sugar. No acid, no rise.

Baking powder is soda with the acid already in it, plus cornstarch to stop it clumping. It works with nothing but heat and wet, which is why it's the safe one and soda is the one that bites you.

They're not interchangeable. A teaspoon of soda wants about three teaspoons of powder to replace it, and in the other direction there's no rule worth the risk — use a recipe.

The test if you suspect it's dead: a spoonful in hot water. Powder should fizz now; soda should fizz when you add vinegar.

