2027-01-10T20:56:32  message   To the owner:
Asked this on Tuesday — short version, then the one thing I still need from you.

The two that actually cause a 4am waking rather than a 4am restlessness: alcohol, because it sedates you for three or four hours and then rebounds you awake — even two drinks will do it to some people. And caffeine, which has a five-hour half-life, so a 3pm coffee is still a quarter of itself in your blood at 4am.

The one behavioural thing that works when you do surface: don't lie there. Fifteen minutes awake in bed and your brain learns the bed is for being awake. Get up, dim light, boring chair, back when you're sleepy. It's unpleasant for a week and then it isn't.

Longer-term: sleep restriction — six and a half hours in bed, same wake time regardless — deepens sleep enough that the 4am surfacing often stops on its own. It's the one with real evidence behind it and it's miserable for the first fortnight.

And rule out reflux and apnoea once, because both wake people in the small hours and neither responds to any of the above.

Now the question that would change all of this: when you wake at 4, is it sudden — something's disturbed you, heart going — or do you just surface, clear-headed, and find yourself awake? Those are different problems and only one of them is about sleep at all.

